WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.807
Have you ever wondered how a prediction inside a computer
becomes a robot moving across a room?

00:00:06.977 --> 00:00:14.891
A model cannot see, remember, calculate, or turn a wheel without
physical components doing those jobs.

00:00:15.061 --> 00:00:21.717
Today we will follow one observation through the hardware, all
the way to an action and new feedback.

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:27.903
Open the Blue Impulse study guide at page thirty-five, Hardware
gives software a body.

00:00:28.073 --> 00:00:35.391
Keep page forty-four nearby too. It explains why a control
signal and motor power are different things.

00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:42.763
You will trace the sensing, memory, processing, and actuation
path of a small robot.

00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:49.973
You will explain why memory movement can limit a fast processor
and what a pipeline actually improves.

00:00:50.143 --> 00:00:57.140
You will also choose hardware by its job, rather than assuming
every AI system needs the same chip.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:02.515
Welcome to the tutorial on how hardware enables AI by Blue
Impulse.

00:01:02.685 --> 00:01:10.237
We are using the guide as our lesson: read the pictured
excerpts, then follow the demonstrations beside them.

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:18.641
Page thirty-five groups hardware by the job it performs: input,
processing, output, and storage.

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:26.939
For our robot, the sensor supplies observations. Memory holds
inputs, model parameters, and working results.

00:01:27.109 --> 00:01:33.872
The processor evaluates the program or model, and an output
communicates the resulting decision.

00:01:34.042 --> 00:01:40.015
If the robot needs to move, an actuator must turn that decision
into physical motion.

00:01:40.185 --> 00:01:47.673
Notice that one device can have multiple roles. A touchscreen
both displays output and senses input.

00:01:47.843 --> 00:01:55.289
The diagram is a chain of responsibilities, not a claim that
every box must be a separate physical chip.

00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:01.596
Use a kitchen to remember the memory hierarchy on page
thirty-six.

00:02:01.766 --> 00:02:05.286
Registers are like the tiny workspace immediately in your

00:02:05.396 --> 00:02:08.895
hands. Cache keeps useful ingredients close to the cook.

00:02:09.065 --> 00:02:15.827
Main memory is the larger active workbench, while persistent
storage is more like the pantry.

00:02:15.997 --> 00:02:21.971
The analogy is about access and capacity, not a literal map of
computer components.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:30.077
A huge pantry does not help if ingredients arrive too slowly.
Similarly, a processor can wait for data.

00:02:30.247 --> 00:02:38.055
Capacity asks how much fits. Memory bandwidth asks how quickly
data can move. These are different questions.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.509
Look at the final paragraph on page thirty-six. Model weights,

00:02:43.619 --> 00:02:46.926
inputs, and intermediate results must fit somewhere.

00:02:47.096 --> 00:02:49.549
When memory bandwidth is insufficient,

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:55.014
a fast processor may spend time waiting instead of doing useful
arithmetic.

00:02:55.184 --> 00:03:01.904
Adding storage capacity alone does not automatically remove a
memory-transfer bottleneck.

00:03:02.074 --> 00:03:06.021
The table also distinguishes cache, main RAM,

00:03:06.131 --> 00:03:11.336
and secondary storage. They are not interchangeable names for
one resource.

00:03:11.506 --> 00:03:19.357
Both static RAM and dynamic RAM are volatile, although their
circuits and refresh requirements differ.

00:03:19.527 --> 00:03:22.855
For this tutorial, focus on the system question:

00:03:22.965 --> 00:03:27.274
is the limiting factor how much fits, or how quickly it arrives?

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.987
Now compare the processing roles in the table on page forty-six.

00:03:33.157 --> 00:03:36.698
A CPU handles general control, branches,

00:03:36.808 --> 00:03:43.123
and diverse tasks. A GPU can accelerate suitable parallel
arithmetic workloads.

00:03:43.293 --> 00:03:48.733
Specialised accelerators can efficiently perform supported
tensor operations,

00:03:48.843 --> 00:03:51.915
but their benefits depend on the actual workload.

00:03:52.085 --> 00:04:01.365
Simple tabular reinforcement learning can run on an ordinary
CPU. AI does not always require a GPU.

00:04:01.535 --> 00:04:08.063
Training changes model parameters. Inference uses an existing
model to produce an output.

00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:16.041
A model can be trained on a large server and deployed on a
smaller device if the deployment requirements fit.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:24.953
Page thirty-nine uses laundry to explain pipelining. One load
can dry while a later load is washing.

00:04:25.123 --> 00:04:30.777
In our simplified processor, the stages are fetch, decode,
execute, and write.

00:04:30.947 --> 00:04:38.733
Instruction one enters fetch in cycle one. In cycle two, it
decodes while instruction two enters fetch.

00:04:38.903 --> 00:04:43.362
Keep stepping forward. The instructions occupy different stages,

00:04:43.472 --> 00:04:47.355
so several instructions make progress during the same cycle.

00:04:47.525 --> 00:04:55.269
After the pipeline fills, this ideal example completes one
instruction per cycle, assuming no stalls.

00:04:55.439 --> 00:05:02.393
It does not make an individual four-stage instruction take just
one cycle from start to finish.

00:05:02.563 --> 00:05:07.299
Real pipelines can pause for dependencies, uncertain branches,

00:05:07.409 --> 00:05:11.463
or shared resources. The guide names these as hazards.

00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:19.142
Use the included Python table to compare completion cycles
rather than relying only on the moving picture.

00:05:20.267 --> 00:05:24.939
Turn to page forty-four and trace the environmental control
loop.

00:05:25.109 --> 00:05:33.365
An analogue sensor may need an analogue-to-digital converter.
Many sensors already provide digital measurements.

00:05:33.535 --> 00:05:40.596
The processor evaluates a decision, then a driver or power stage
supplies what the actuator needs.

00:05:40.766 --> 00:05:46.014
Some actuators use a digital interface or pulse-width
modulation.

00:05:46.124 --> 00:05:50.007
They do not all require a digital-to-analogue converter.

00:05:50.177 --> 00:05:58.369
Pulse-width modulation is timed switching, not the same thing as
converting a value into an analogue voltage.

00:05:58.539 --> 00:06:05.237
A control signal can tell a motor what to do without being able
to supply the current that moves it.

00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:13.513
Imagine the robot detects an obstacle, selects a turn, and sends
a command to its motor driver.

00:06:13.683 --> 00:06:20.126
The driver powers the motor, the wheels move, and the sensor
measures the changed situation.

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:27.187
That final measurement closes the feedback loop. The command
alone does not prove the movement succeeded.

00:06:27.357 --> 00:06:33.842
A wheel might slip, a sensor might be noisy, or the actuator
might reach a mechanical limit.

00:06:34.012 --> 00:06:39.687
The book also separates monitoring from control. Displaying a
measurement monitors;

00:06:39.797 --> 00:06:42.570
changing the environment adds control.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:49.673
This loop can use a fixed rule or a learned policy. The hardware
path alone does not tell you which.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.112
Pause and diagnose a system. Its processor repeatedly waits for
data,

00:06:56.222 --> 00:06:59.443
and its motor driver cannot supply enough current.

00:06:59.613 --> 00:07:07.059
Would replacing the processor with a faster GPU solve both
problems? Think before the answer appears.

00:07:13.229 --> 00:07:21.229
No. Memory movement and actuator power are separate limits that
a new GPU does not automatically remove.

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:27.543
Measure the path, identify the limiting stage, and match the
intervention to that evidence.

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:33.921
Page eighty-six broadens the discussion to power, heat, memory,
and physical constraints.

00:07:34.091 --> 00:07:38.912
An advertised arithmetic rate describes one capability. It is

00:07:39.022 --> 00:07:43.139
not a complete measurement of the robot's useful performance.

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:48.661
Recap the path from page thirty-five: observations enter,

00:07:48.771 --> 00:07:55.235
memory holds information, processing evaluates it, and outputs
communicate or act.

00:07:55.405 --> 00:07:58.691
Page thirty-six explains why memory capacity

00:07:58.801 --> 00:08:05.499
and bandwidth both matter. Page thirty-nine separates pipeline
throughput from instruction latency.

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:09.723
Page forty-four explains conversion, control signals,

00:08:09.833 --> 00:08:14.526
and actuator power. Page forty-six compares processing
strengths.

00:08:14.696 --> 00:08:19.880
Choose hardware by the workload, deadlines, power budget, and
physical task.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:24.487
To check your understanding, draw a robot's path from camera to
wheel,

00:08:24.597 --> 00:08:28.032
and label one possible bottleneck at each stage.

00:08:28.202 --> 00:08:34.175
Then explain why a more powerful chip does not, by itself,
create a learning algorithm.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:40.207
This tutorial used the Blue Impulse study guide, pages
thirty-five,

00:08:40.317 --> 00:08:44.797
thirty-six, thirty-nine, forty-four, forty-six, and eighty-six.

00:08:44.967 --> 00:08:48.700
The original book cover is shown on screen as our credit.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:51.174
The companion tutorial PDF

00:08:51.284 --> 00:08:57.684
and pipeline demonstration help you repeat the lesson without
replaying every explanation.

00:08:57.854 --> 00:09:03.038
Blue Impulse study guide. Trace the whole system before choosing
the hardware.
